  "body": "A release-mode constructor probe on ddb7199d7f0b0f04390e923474b630ffc7ff2811 accepts `Packed::<32>::new(usize::MAX / 32 + 1)`. Its `count * N` wraps to zero, allocating no lines while retaining the huge count. Safe `Deref`, `DerefMut` and `as_blocks` later construct slices from that count. Their allocation/size invariant no longer holds.\n\nThe diagnostic only constructs and drops the object. **No invalid slice or memory access was executed.** A desired fail-stop assertion is red; small ordinary inputs are unaffected. The implicated block.rs is byte-identical on current next fbfb319f3414ac7fac4b3d4bce266488a1fdee3b. Release-version exposure is not yet triaged.\n\nPlausible harm: an untrusted record/message count gets through an ordinary safe constructor, bypasses Vec's capacity rejection and permits UB through safe methods. The docs state no count precondition that makes this acceptable.\n\nSmallest remedy to review: compute line count as `count.div_ceil(128 / N)` after existing FITS, letting Vec's existing checked allocation reject impossible capacity. This avoids the overflowing byte product without a new API/state/policy. Add release controls for N=16/32 and the wrap boundary.\n\nPinned evidence: safety-cursory-probes-epyc-20261108T144442-53674417; source unchanged. The run verifies the expected red capacity assertion, not a passing library safety gate. Full script/raw publication follows. Please treat as a safety blocker pending review.\n\n\u2014 Sam Shackleton",
